    "primary_value_col": "Background",
    "values": {
      "Background": "The Trans-Arabian Pipeline (Tapline) was a crude oil pipeline running from Qaisumah in Saudi Arabia across Jordan, southern Syria (including the Golan Heights), and into Lebanon, terminating at the Sidon (Zahrani) Mediterranean export terminal. Constructed 1947-1950 by the Trans-Arabian Pipeline Company (a subsidiary of Aramco) using 30-inch and 31-inch steel pipe over a 1,213-1,214 km route, with six main pumping stations (Nariya, Qaisumah, Rafha, Badanah, Turaif in Saudi Arabia; Qaryatain in Jordan). Initial capacity 320,000 bpd, expanded via auxiliary pumping in 1957 to ~450,000 bpd and a peak nameplate of ~500,000 bpd. The cross-border line ceased operating in 1976 as the Lebanese Civil War began and supertankers eroded its economics; the Saudi and Jordanian portions continued intermittently until 1983/1990. Final 'de-cruding' (cleaning of residual crude) was completed in 2001. Designated Saudi Arabia's first industrial heritage site in December 2020."
    },
    "primary_value": "The Trans-Arabian Pipeline (Tapline) was a crude oil pipeline running from Qaisumah in Saudi Arabia across Jordan, southern Syria (including the Golan Heights), and into Lebanon, terminating at the Sidon (Zahrani) Mediterranean export terminal. Constructed 1947-1950 by the Trans-Arabian Pipeline Company (a subsidiary of Aramco) using 30-inch and 31-inch steel pipe over a 1,213-1,214 km route, with six main pumping stations (Nariya, Qaisumah, Rafha, Badanah, Turaif in Saudi Arabia; Qaryatain in Jordan). Initial capacity 320,000 bpd, expanded via auxiliary pumping in 1957 to ~450,000 bpd and a peak nameplate of ~500,000 bpd. The cross-border line ceased operating in 1976 as the Lebanese Civil War began and supertankers eroded its economics; the Saudi and Jordanian portions continued intermittently until 1983/1990. Final 'de-cruding' (cleaning of residual crude) was completed in 2001. Designated Saudi Arabia's first industrial heritage site in December 2020.",
    "current_ref": "https://www.aramco.com/en/news-media/elements-magazine/2021/the-tapline-a-legacy-of-triumph, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trans-Arabian_Pipeline, https://www.britannica.com/topic/Trans-Arabian-Pipeline, https://saudipedia.com/en/article/941/economy-and-business/energy-and-natural-resources/trans-arabian-pipeline-tapline",
